From: Escapement
Subject: [PATCH] flash: chunk writes, stage them through internal DRAM, instrument cache-disables

Three changes to the firmware tree, all about the same failure: a flash write
from core 0 reboots the board while Linux is running.

CHUNKING. linux_flash.c set g_spi_flash_skip_ipc around each esp_flash_* call.
That flag is correct -- the IPC that would park the other core can never
complete, because core 1 is Linux and not a FreeRTOS task (JFS-001) -- but the
call that follows still runs
spi_flash_disable_interrupts_caches_and_other_cpu(), which disables non-IRAM
interrupts, suspends the scheduler and turns the cache off for the WHOLE
request. One 32 KB write is one uninterruptible window, and the interrupt
watchdog resets the chip when that runs long. Reproduced with nothing else
running: 1 KB survives, 32 KB reboots with rst reason 5 (ESP_RST_INT_WDT).
After chunking to 4096 bytes the longest single flash call measures 55.6 ms and
32/64/128 KB writes all complete.

STAGING THROUGH INTERNAL DRAM -- this is the actual fix. Chunking bounded the
window but did not stop the reboot, because the window is not a watchdog
problem. The cache is SHARED on this chip (SOC_IDCACHE_PER_CORE is defined for
esp32 and NOT for esp32s3, so the other_cpuid branch in cache_utils.c is
compiled out): disabling it on core 0 disables it for the whole chip. The
kernel is XIP -- CONFIG_XIP_KERNEL=y, _stext at 0x42140000 -- so core 1 fetches
kernel instructions through that cache and dies on the first fetch.

The fatal access, though, was on core 0. The write source is Linux's buffer and
therefore lives in PSRAM, and esp_flash_write() read it while the cache was
off. Each 4096-byte chunk is now memcpy'd into a static internal-DRAM buffer
with the cache still on, and esp_flash_write() reads the copy.

Measured on hardware 2026-11-11, emulator playing throughout: four consecutive
1 KiB writes to /home (5 cache-disable windows each) all survived; a 128 KiB
write plus 40 file create/delete cycles (891 windows) survived; nine rounds of
64 KiB write+delete over 100 s (1860 windows) survived. Before this change a
single 1 KiB write rebooted the board every single time, at /home 74 percent
full and again at 47 percent full.

INSTRUMENTATION. esp-idf's spi_flash/cache_utils.c counts every call to
spi_flash_disable_interrupts_caches_and_other_cpu() after the Linux handoff and
records the caller, in INTERNAL DRAM -- never in PSRAM, because this function
runs with the cache off and a PSRAM store from it is fatal. An earlier version
stored to 0x3df70110 and stopped the board booting with no output at all; that
address is also 16 bytes inside SHM_DIAG_PREV_OFF, so it corrupted the crash
snapshot it existed to help read. main/esc_instr.c polls the counters and
publishes them into the window once the window is mapped and arming has
happened. The previous boot's record is copied to SHM_DIAG_FLASH_PREV_OFF in
linux_boot.c, the one place that can safely read the window before this boot
overwrites it.

Apply the firmware half from build/esp-hosted/esp_hosted_ng and the IDF half
inside esp/esp_driver/esp-idf. Both trees are LOCAL ONLY and never pushed.

--- linux_boot.c / linux_flash.c / app_main.c / CMakeLists.txt (firmware tree) ---
diff --git a/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/app_main.c b/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/app_main.c
index 8664ab1..33e5fd8 100644
--- a/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/app_main.c
+++ b/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/app_main.c
@@ -739,6 +739,14 @@ void app_main()
 	void tamacore_start(void);
 	tamacore_start();
 
+	/*
+	 * Publishes the flash-path instrument's counters into the shared
+	 * window once the window is mapped. Must be created before the handoff
+	 * because linux_boot() never returns; it is inert until armed.
+	 */
+	void esc_instr_start(void);
+	esc_instr_start();
+
 	void linux_boot(void);
 	linux_boot();
 }
diff --git a/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/CMakeLists.txt b/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/CMakeLists.txt
index ee81530..3af223c 100644
--- a/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -1,6 +1,6 @@
 set(COMPONENT_SRCS "app_main.c" "slave_bt.c" "cmd.c" "stats.c" "linux_boot.c" "linux_ipc.c" "linux_flash.c" "ble_prov.c" "ble_hid.c" "log_pipe.c" "bond.c"
     # the Tamagotchi runs here now, on core 0 -- see tamacore_task.c
-    "tamacore_task.c" "splb32.c" "ht24lc08.c" "con_v3_ir.c" "splb32_sound.c" "segmap.c")
+    "tamacore_task.c" "esc_instr.c" "splb32.c" "ht24lc08.c" "con_v3_ir.c" "splb32_sound.c" "segmap.c")
 set(COMPONENT_ADD_INCLUDEDIRS "./include" ".")
 
 if(CONFIG_IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3)
diff --git a/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/linux_boot.c b/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/linux_boot.c
index 0406af3..42f2598 100644
--- a/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/linux_boot.c
+++ b/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/linux_boot.c
@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@
 #include "freertos/task.h"
 #include "tiny_jffs2_reader.h"
 #include "bootparam.h"
+#include "shmproto.h"
 #include "linux_ll.h"
 
 static const esp_partition_t *find_partition(const char *name)
@@ -105,18 +106,34 @@ static void map_flash_and_go(void)
 	init_linux_mmap();
 
 	/*
-	 * Arm the flash-path instrument (see the LOCAL INSTRUMENTATION note in
-	 * esp-idf's spi_flash/cache_utils.c). From here on, any flash operation
-	 * on core 0 turns the external-memory cache off for the whole chip while
-	 * Linux is executing from PSRAM with no cache of its own -- which kills
-	 * core 1. The counter records whether that ever happens.
+	 * Preserve the PREVIOUS boot's flash instrument reading before this boot
+	 * overwrites it. This is the only point in the firmware that can do so
+	 * safely: the window is mapped by init_linux_mmap() just above, and the
+	 * caches are on, so a PSRAM read here is legal. Inside the instrument
+	 * itself it would not be -- see the note in esp-idf's
+	 * spi_flash/cache_utils.c.
+	 *
+	 * Without this the counter restarts at zero every boot, so the one
+	 * number worth having -- how many cache-disable windows had happened
+	 * when the board died -- is destroyed by the reset that made it
+	 * interesting. That is exactly how the earlier instrument produced a
+	 * suspicious 25 and proved nothing.
 	 */
 	{
-		volatile uint32_t *n = (volatile uint32_t *)0x3df70110u;
-		volatile uint32_t *live = (volatile uint32_t *)0x3df70118u;
-
-		*n = 0;
-		*live = 0x45534331u;    /* "ESC1" */
+		struct shm_flash_instr *live =
+			(struct shm_flash_instr *)(SHM_BASE + SHM_DIAG_FLASH_OFF);
+		struct shm_flash_instr *prev =
+			(struct shm_flash_instr *)(SHM_BASE + SHM_DIAG_FLASH_PREV_OFF);
+
+		if (live->magic == SHM_DIAG_FLASH_MAGIC) {
+			prev->magic = live->magic;
+			prev->calls = live->calls;
+			prev->ret = live->ret;
+			prev->cpu = live->cpu;
+			prev->armed = live->armed;
+			prev->max_cyc = live->max_cyc;
+			prev->last_cyc = live->last_cyc;
+		}
 	}
 
 	part = find_partition("linux");
@@ -136,6 +153,27 @@ static void map_flash_and_go(void)
 	extern int g_abort_on_ipc;
 	g_abort_on_ipc = 1;
 
+	/*
+	 * Arm the flash-path instrument here, last: the window is mapped by now
+	 * (init_linux_mmap above), so the publisher task in esc_instr.c can
+	 * legally copy these into PSRAM, and anything counted from this point on
+	 * happened with Linux about to run on core 1.
+	 *
+	 * These live in internal DRAM, not in the window: this function and the
+	 * one it arms are both reachable with the caches off.
+	 */
+	{
+		extern volatile uint32_t g_esc_flash_calls;
+		extern volatile uint32_t g_esc_flash_ret;
+		extern volatile uint32_t g_esc_flash_cpu;
+		extern volatile uint32_t g_esc_flash_armed;
+
+		g_esc_flash_calls = 0;
+		g_esc_flash_ret = 0;
+		g_esc_flash_cpu = 0;
+		g_esc_flash_armed = 1;
+	}
+
 	asm volatile ("mov a2, %1 ; jx %0" :: "r"(linux), "r"(tag) : "a2");
 }
 
diff --git a/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/linux_flash.c b/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/linux_flash.c
index 288c0d5..c540bbb 100644
--- a/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/linux_flash.c
+++ b/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/network_adapter/main/linux_flash.c
@@ -47,6 +47,28 @@ extern int g_spi_flash_skip_ipc;
  */
 #define FLASH_CHUNK	4096u
 
+/*
+ * Staging buffer for writes, in INTERNAL DRAM.
+ *
+ * The write source is Linux's buffer, so it lives in PSRAM. PSRAM is reached
+ * through the external-memory cache, and this handler calls esp_flash_write()
+ * with that cache disabled (via g_spi_flash_skip_ipc -> the cache-disable path
+ * in cache_utils.c). Reading a PSRAM source inside that window is therefore
+ * exactly as illegal as storing to PSRAM from it.
+ *
+ * So each chunk is copied into internal DRAM with the cache still ON, and
+ * esp_flash_write() reads the copy. Nothing on this core touches PSRAM between
+ * the cache going off and coming back.
+ *
+ * This is an experiment with a specific expected result: if a 1 KiB write to
+ * /home during emulator play stops rebooting the board, the fatal read was
+ * this one and it was on core 0 all along rather than on core 1.
+ *
+ * 4 KiB of internal DRAM, and a static array rather than a malloc because the
+ * heap can fragment while this must always be available.
+ */
+static uint8_t flash_stage[FLASH_CHUNK] __attribute__((aligned(4)));
+
 /*
  * Flash work is recorded into the shared diagnostics, which live in PSRAM and
  * survive a reset. That is the only way to find out what the board was doing
@@ -97,27 +119,28 @@ static void esp_flash_rx(void *p, void *data)
 			asm volatile ("memw" ::: "memory");
 		}
 		/*
-		 * CHUNKED, and this is the fix for the reboots.
+		 * CHUNKED. This bounds each uninterruptible window; it is NOT the
+		 * fix for the reboots, and an earlier version of this comment
+		 * claimed it was.
 		 *
 		 * With g_spi_flash_skip_ipc set, each esp_flash_* call runs with
 		 * non-IRAM interrupts disabled, the scheduler suspended and the
 		 * cache off for its WHOLE duration -- see
 		 * spi_flash_disable_interrupts_caches_and_other_cpu() in
-		 * cache_utils.c. The interrupt watchdog resets the chip if that
-		 * state lasts too long, and a 32 KB jffs2 write reproducibly
-		 * rebooted this board with rst reason 5 (ESP_RST_INT_WDT).
+		 * cache_utils.c. One call over a whole 32 KB request is a long
+		 * window, so the request is split into FLASH_CHUNK-sized pieces
+		 * and the flag is dropped between them, letting esp_flash_*
+		 * re-enable interrupts on each return. Erase must be
+		 * sector-aligned, and 4096 is both the flash sector and jffs2's
+		 * erase size here, so one size works for both.
 		 *
-		 * One call over the whole request is therefore not safe, and the
-		 * fact that a 1 KB write survives while 32 KB does not is the
-		 * measurement that says so.
-		 *
-		 * So the request is split into FLASH_CHUNK-sized pieces and the
-		 * flag is dropped between them, which lets esp_flash_* re-enable
-		 * interrupts on each return. Each uninterruptible window is then
-		 * bounded by one chunk regardless of how large the write is.
-		 * Erase must be sector-aligned, and 4096 is both the flash sector
-		 * and jffs2's erase size here, so the same chunk size works for
-		 * both.
+		 * The reboots were a different fault and chunking did not change
+		 * them: a 1 KB write -- a single chunk -- rebooted the board just
+		 * as reliably as 32 KB did, while the longest window measured
+		 * 0.51 ms against an 800 ms watchdog. The real cause was this
+		 * handler passing a PSRAM source buffer to esp_flash_write() with
+		 * the cache off, which the staging buffer above now avoids.
+		 * Bounding the window is still worth doing on its own merits.
 		 */
 		switch (cmd->code) {
 		case ESP_IPC_FLASH_CMD_ERASE: {
@@ -160,8 +183,11 @@ static void esp_flash_rx(void *p, void *data)
 
 				int64_t t0 = esp_timer_get_time();
 
+				/* PSRAM -> internal DRAM, with the cache on. */
+				memcpy(flash_stage, cmd->data + done, n);
+
 				g_spi_flash_skip_ipc = 1;
-				ret = esp_flash_write(NULL, cmd->data + done,
+				ret = esp_flash_write(NULL, flash_stage,
 						      cmd->addr + done, n);
 				g_spi_flash_skip_ipc = 0;
 				flash_record(cmd->code, n, esp_timer_get_time() - t0);

--- esp-idf spi_flash/cache_utils.c (nested submodule) ---
diff --git a/components/spi_flash/cache_utils.c b/components/spi_flash/cache_utils.c
index d235a41e..9e82c249 100644
--- a/components/spi_flash/cache_utils.c
+++ b/components/spi_flash/cache_utils.c
@@ -151,8 +151,66 @@ void IRAM_ATTR spi_flash_op_block_func(void *arg)
 
 int g_spi_flash_skip_ipc;
 
+/*
+ * LOCAL INSTRUMENTATION (Escapement), never upstream.
+ *
+ * Counts every call to this function after core 0 arms it at the Linux
+ * handoff, and records the caller. This is the only place that catches EVERY
+ * cache-disabling flash operation, including ones this project does not own;
+ * instrumenting our own linux_flash.c handler instead left a board that froze
+ * with that handler idle and unexplained.
+ *
+ * THE COUNTERS ARE IN INTERNAL DRAM AND THIS FUNCTION TOUCHES NOTHING ELSE.
+ *
+ * That is not a style choice. This function is called during boot, before
+ * core 0's MMU maps the shared PSRAM window, and it is always called with the
+ * external-memory cache disabled -- so a store to PSRAM from here is both
+ * invalid and fatal. An earlier version of this instrument guarded itself on a
+ * word at 0x3df70118 and stored at 0x3df70110. The board stopped booting with
+ * no output at all, because the firmware console is UART0 and nothing reaches
+ * the USB port, and 0x70110 is inside SHM_DIAG_PREV_OFF -- the crash snapshot
+ * it was supposed to help read. Two bugs, one symptom.
+ *
+ * main/esc_instr.c owns everything downstream of this: it polls these four
+ * words and copies them into the window once the window is usable.
+ *
+ * A flash operation here turns the external-memory cache off for the whole
+ * chip. Linux on core 1 executes from PSRAM with no cache of its own, so any
+ * pass through this point while core 1 is running can kill it. `calls` says
+ * whether it happens at all; `ret` says who.
+ */
+volatile uint32_t g_esc_flash_calls;
+volatile uint32_t g_esc_flash_ret;
+volatile uint32_t g_esc_flash_cpu;
+volatile uint32_t g_esc_flash_armed;
+volatile uint32_t g_esc_flash_max_cyc;
+volatile uint32_t g_esc_flash_last_cyc;
+static volatile uint32_t g_esc_flash_t0;
+
+/* CCOUNT is a register read: legal with the cache off and interrupts disabled,
+ * and it needs no include, which matters because this file is built for every
+ * target and the surrounding includes are all #if'd by target. */
+static inline uint32_t esc_ccount(void)
+{
+    uint32_t c;
+
+    __asm__ volatile ("rsr %0, ccount" : "=r"(c));
+    return c;
+}
+
 void IRAM_ATTR spi_flash_disable_interrupts_caches_and_other_cpu(void)
 {
+    /* Internal RAM only, and cheap enough to leave in: three stores and a
+     * load. Deliberately before the assert, so a call that would abort is
+     * still counted. */
+    if (g_esc_flash_armed) {
+        g_esc_flash_ret = (uint32_t)(uintptr_t)__builtin_return_address(0);
+        g_esc_flash_cpu = (uint32_t)xPortGetCoreID();
+        /* Cycle counter is a register read: legal with the cache off. */
+        g_esc_flash_t0 = esc_ccount();
+        g_esc_flash_calls = g_esc_flash_calls + 1;
+    }
+
     assert(esp_task_stack_is_sane_cache_disabled());
 
     spi_flash_op_lock();
@@ -219,6 +277,20 @@ void IRAM_ATTR spi_flash_enable_interrupts_caches_and_other_cpu(void)
 {
     const int cpuid = xPortGetCoreID();
 
+    /*
+     * Close the window measurement before anything is re-enabled, so the
+     * duration covers the whole no-interrupts, no-cache interval. This is the
+     * number compared against CONFIG_ESP_INT_WDT_TIMEOUT_MS.
+     */
+    if (g_esc_flash_armed && g_esc_flash_t0) {
+        uint32_t d = esc_ccount() - g_esc_flash_t0;
+
+        g_esc_flash_t0 = 0;
+        g_esc_flash_last_cyc = d;
+        if (d > g_esc_flash_max_cyc)
+            g_esc_flash_max_cyc = d;
+    }
+
 #ifndef NDEBUG
     // Sanity check: flash operation ends on the same CPU as it has started
     assert(cpuid == s_flash_op_cpu);
